WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.135 --> 00:00:02.760
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome to "Cloud
Security Basics," a new series

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.890
where we explain the
ins and outs of securing

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.080
your application
on Google Cloud.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:07.660
Sound fun?

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.790
Then stick around,
because in this episode,

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.060
you'll learn about the first
of three distinct areas

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.885
of cloud security risk--

00:00:13.885 --> 00:00:14.385
access.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.119
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.800
BRAD ACTOR: Hello,
cloud detective.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.530
You showed that you understand
cloud's shared security model,

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.480
but can you solve this?

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.580
What can you do to
stop the wrong people

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.250
from accessing your resources?

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.560
For example, I could use
a man-in-the-middle attack

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.440
to intrude on an
existing connection,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.860
intercepting and modifying all
network traffic between two

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.580
devices.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.400
If I intercepted
an admin's traffic,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.860
I'd even be able to
act on your system

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.440
with all the privileges
of your admin.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.870
What can you do to stop that?

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.600
For your sake, I hope
you know the answer.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.870
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
here's what we know.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.510
A man-in-the-middle attack
involves an attacker getting

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.390
access to credentials
they shouldn't have.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.940
We could solve this,
but there's actually

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.620
a broader problem here--

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.400
access.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.880
Access represents ensuring that
only the right identities have

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.670
access to the right
resources for your system.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.740
For example, you might
not want developers

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.110
to be able to delete
resources on your system,

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.290
so you have to enact a
policy, or access control,

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.410
to prevent that from happening
by accident or ill will.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.290
Or you might want to prevent
malicious access exploits

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.600
like phishing, where an
attacker tricks individuals

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.040
into giving out
their credentials,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.398
or denial of service attacks,
where an attacker works

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:48.940
to overwhelm a web
service to keep it

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.710
from functioning properly.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.010
Luckily, Google Cloud provides
a lot of tools and services

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.210
to protect its users.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.600
Some of those protections
include encrypted traffic

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.240
and load balancers to combat
man-in-the-middle and DDoS

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.290
exploits, universal two-factor
authentication to minimize

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.940
phishing, Cloud Identity-Aware
Proxy to make sure that only

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.900
the right people get
access to applications,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.200
endpoint management to make
sure that users are using secure

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.470
devices to access company
resources, and more.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.520
Let's see how they work.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.240
So how do these tools
keep the wrong people

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.070
from accessing your system?

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.380
I know that to combat
man-in-the-middle and DDoS

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.290
exploits, Google Cloud
encrypts all internet access

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.290
at the network level by
default, eliminating the concern

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:37.807
of a passive adversary listening
for sensitive information.

00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:38.640
Let me show you how.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.530
Since TLS requires that
servers prove their identity,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.190
active man-in-the-middle attacks
are also much more difficult.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.130
And Google Cloud's front end
and load balancers support TLS

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.580
termination while providing an
entry point to Google's massive

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.240
serving resources, making a
successful DDoS attack quite

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.810
challenging.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.060
Here's how it works.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.070
Load balancers
report information

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.490
about incoming traffic to
a central DDoS service.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.820
If it detects that a
DDoS attack is occurring,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.440
it can configure the load
balancers to drop or throttle

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.610
the traffic.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.380
Google's central
identity service,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.810
which users see as
the Google login page,

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.790
asks for a user
name and password

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.860
and assesses risk factors
such as whether users

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.130
have logged in from the same
device or location in the past.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.380
The service issues
credentials, such as cookies

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.660
and OAuth tokens, to
further protect users.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.260
Oh, and second factors,
such as one-time passwords

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.300
or phishing-resistant
security keys,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.940
can also be used by users
when signing in to help

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.560
prevent phishing.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.590
In fact, in order to make
phishing less effective,

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.370
Google pioneered the
U2F Titan Security Key,

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.840
a hardware second factor
that can be associated

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.480
with an account so that
login is only possible when

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.220
the second factor is present--

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:53.260
the idea being if
you pair something

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.590
you know, like a password,
with something you have,

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.800
like a security key, phishing
becomes very difficult.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.318
And it's easy to set up, too.

00:04:01.318 --> 00:04:02.860
Once you set these
keys up and change

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:05.500
the two-step authentication
to only use security keys

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.870
in the Google Admin
UI, then the next time

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.640
a developer logs on, after
they enter their password,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.310
they'll be asked to
touch their security key.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:13.810
Hmm.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.600
How else can we limit
unauthorized access?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.810
What about Cloud
Identity-Aware Proxy, or IAP?

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.990
IAP makes it easy for you to put
up an application on the cloud

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.790
and configure a
central policy users

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.860
have to authenticate against.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.100
Best of all, since IAP is
enforced at the network layer,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.590
Cloud IAP requires little to
no application code changes.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.670
So if you had a legacy
application that

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.810
was hosted on-premise
behind a firewall,

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.300
and wanted to migrate it to a
public endpoint on the cloud,

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.180
you could drop Cloud
IAP in front of it

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.680
to require authentication and
authorization against a Google

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.440
Group or G Suite domain
with little to no changes

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.390
to the application at all.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:51.220
But that still
leaves the case of,

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.130
what if the laptop of an admin
is compromised by malware?

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.500
Hmm.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.420
That's where endpoints
management comes in.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.670
If your platform can be
accessed from your employee's

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.710
own personal phone
or tablet, you

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:04.450
can use G Suite
Endpoint Management

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.420
to keep your company data
safe while letting employees

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.520
use their favorite
personal device.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.650
And with endpoints, it
becomes easy to do things

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:12.820
like set up policies.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:14.860
Like, on Android
devices, work apps

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.260
will be separated
from personal apps,

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:21.270
or since Chrome OS is designed
for security from the ground up

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.770
and doesn't allow the
installation of software,

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:29.280
only use admin access if the
admin is using a Chromebook.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.090
I figured it out!

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:33.052
I've got the answer!

00:05:33.052 --> 00:05:34.260
BRAD ACTOR: Hello, detective.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.670
I see you've been busy.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.560
You've answered my
question-- what can you

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.080
do to stop the wrong people
from accessing your data?

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.410
You noticed that Google Cloud
could help secure access

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.270
and authentication.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.000
Google Cloud provides help
through Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.468
endpoint verification,
universal two-factor, and more.

00:05:51.468 --> 00:05:53.010
All of these features
are building up

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.320
context associated
with the access

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.360
to make sure it
follows the rules.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.210
The access should come
from a Chromebook,

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.437
the user should have proper
credentials to log in,

00:06:01.437 --> 00:06:03.270
they should have the
required access levels,

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.090
and they need to have a
hardware second factor in order

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.760
to get to the particular API.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.910
These individual protections
layer on top of each other

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.740
to help keep your cloud
safer and more secure.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.770
Very good, very good.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.240
You've answered
today's question,

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.130
but be ready,
because next time I

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.488
want to see what you can
do to keep your data safe.

00:06:23.488 --> 00:06:25.030
CARTER MORGAN: So
there you have it--

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.660
another episode of
"Cloud Security Basics."

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.510
Next episode will focus on the
second of three distinct areas

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.950
of cloud security risk--

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:32.925
data.

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:34.800
In the meantime, if you
want to take a deeper

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.653
dive into securing
access in the cloud,

00:06:36.653 --> 00:06:38.820
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.250
below, and stay tuned for the
rest of the Cloud Security

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.560
Basics series because
when it comes to security,

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.140
you can't let bad actors win.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

